@page "/privacy"
@page "/privacy-policy"

<PageTitle>Privacy Policy | Comfidiario</PageTitle>
<SeoMetadata Title="Privacy Policy | Comfidiario"
             Description="Learn what information Comfidiario handles and how account and journal data are used."
             CanonicalPath="/privacy"
             Robots="index, follow" />

<main class="legal-page">
    <article class="legal-document">
        <p class="legal-eyebrow">Legal &amp; Privacy</p>
        <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
        <p class="legal-updated">Service information draft — review and complete operator and provider details before public launch.</p>

        <section>
            <h2>Who operates Comfidiario</h2>
            <p>
                Comfidiario is operated by an individual based in Peru. The operator's full legal name and
                postal contact address have not been supplied for this page and must be added before launch.
                “We” in this policy refers to the Comfidiario operator.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Information the service handles</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Account information:</strong> your display name, email address, account creation
                    time, and a password hash when you register with a password. Passwords are hashed; the
                    application does not store the submitted password as plain text.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Sign-in information:</strong> if Google sign-in is enabled, the application uses
                    the name and email returned during sign-in to find or create an account. Google handles
                    its own sign-in flow under its own terms and privacy policy.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Journal information:</strong> journal titles, entries, dates, and page information
                    that you create. Signed-in journal titles and entry text are protected by ASP.NET Core
                    Data Protection before being stored in the application's database. This is not
                    end-to-end encryption: the service can decrypt this information to provide the journal.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Anonymous journal information:</strong> before sign-in, journal data is stored in
                    the browser's local storage on that device. It is not automatically transferred into an
                    account.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Operational information:</strong> the hosting platform and service providers may
                    process connection and diagnostic information needed to run, secure, and troubleshoot the
                    service. The exact log fields and retention periods depend on hosting configuration and
                    have not been confirmed for this page.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>How information is used</h2>
            <p>
                Account information is used to create accounts and authenticate users. Journal information is
                used to save and display your journals. Technical and diagnostic information may be used to
                operate, protect, and troubleshoot the application.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Storage and service providers</h2>
            <p>
                The application uses PostgreSQL for its database. The production database provider and
                processing region are determined by deployment configuration and must be confirmed by the
                operator. In production, Data Protection keys are configured to persist in Azure Blob Storage;
                the application also uses Azure App Service diagnostics. Google sign-in is optional and is
                available only when configured.
            </p>
            <p>
                The application loads some third-party resources, including Google Fonts and GSAP from
                jsDelivr. A browser may contact those providers when loading pages that use those resources.
                Their own privacy terms apply to information they receive.
            </p>
            <p>
                The application source does not show an analytics integration. Hosting, database, CDN, or
                identity-provider logging and processing should be checked in the deployed service settings.
                The locations where those providers process or store information have not been confirmed here.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Cookies and browser storage</h2>
            <p>
                The service uses cookies for authentication and session functionality, and antiforgery tokens
                to protect form submissions. Optional Google sign-in uses a short-lived external sign-in
                cookie when configured. Anonymous journal content is kept in local storage, not a cookie.
                See the <a href="/cookies">Cookie Policy</a> for more detail.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Retention and deletion</h2>
            <p>
                Account and journal records remain in the application database until deleted or otherwise
                removed by the operator. Signed-in users can request deletion using the account deletion
                function in the application; deleting the account also deletes its journals and entries
                through the database's configured relationships. Copies in backups, diagnostic logs, or
                provider systems may remain until their applicable retention and deletion processes remove
                them. Those periods have not been verified and should be confirmed before launch.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Your choices and requests</h2>
            <p>
                You can edit journal information in the application and delete your account from the account
                deletion page. To ask a privacy question or make another data request, contact the operator
                using the address on the <a href="/contact">Contact &amp; Support</a> page. The email shown
                there is currently a placeholder and must be replaced before launch.
            </p>
            <p>
                Privacy requirements may vary depending on where you live and how the service is offered.
                This page does not determine whether a particular law applies or claim compliance with any
                law. The operator should obtain appropriate legal advice and complete the missing provider,
                location, retention, identity, and contact details.
            </p>
        </section>

        <nav class="legal-page-links" aria-label="Other legal pages">
            <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a>
            <a href="/cookies">Cookie Policy</a>
            <a href="/contact">Contact &amp; Support</a>
        </nav>
    </article>
</main>
